      "Language": "ger",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Nephrogenic pruritus (NP) is a well-known associated symptom in patients with chronic renal failure. The aim of the present study was to make a detailed dermatological analysis including distribution of excoriations and their correlation with pruritus characteristics."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "PATIENTS AND METHODS",
          "content": "Data on a total of 17 patients with NP (14 males, mean 65.4\u2009± SD 14.3 years) were subjected to retrospective analysis. Most of the patients developed NP after start of kidney disease; however, in 20\u2009% of the patients, there was premonitory appearance of the symptom 18 months (median) earlier. A majority of patients reported neuropathic symptom qualities (burning, stinging). In 94.1\u2009% of patients xerosis was present; in 58.8\u2009%, prurigo nodularis. The latter group of patients had a longer duration of pruritus as well as up to 10 years longer duration of renal disease than those without prurigo."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Pruritus characteristics of NP show a wide variance without a clear profile that is useful for clinical diagnosis. NP can occur premonitorily, and, if of long duration and with coexistent metabolic diseases, can develop into prurigo."
        }
      ]},
